<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/brain-anatomy-tour">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The brain is <strong>~86 billion neurons in a highly ordered arrangement</strong> — and its
    anatomy is functional: damage to a specific region produces a specific deficit, which is how
    the map was first drawn (by injury, mostly) and confirmed by imaging. This lesson is the
    guided tour the rest of the ladder assumes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the regions, their jobs, and the famous injuries that mapped them" widget="equationStep" config={anatomyCfg} />

  <h2>Landmark cases that taught neuroscience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Phineas Gage (1848):</strong> an iron rod through his frontal lobe; "Gage was no longer Gage" — the first famous link between frontal cortex and personality/decision-making.</li>
    <li><strong>Patient H.M. (1953):</strong> hippocampus removed to treat epilepsy → could not form new memories (but could learn skills). Revealed memory as multiple systems — the memory-circuits lesson covers it.</li>
    <li><strong>Broca and Wernicke (1860s):</strong> two patients with opposite language deficits from opposite brain regions — the first language map, still largely correct.</li>
    <li><strong>Split-brain patients:</strong> cut corpus callosum (epilepsy treatment) revealed hemisphere specialisation live — the left hand "didn't know" what the left hemisphere had seen. Sperry, Nobel 1981.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; MacLean's triune model (with modern caveats); Sperry (1981) Nobel lecture.</p>
</TopicPage>
